  <article class="text-page">

    <h1>THE UNFAIR SIDE OF SCIENCE</h1>

    <p class="meta">
      27 mars 2026 (<a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1W7pjC8iS8fgVsVKIB_vyW8jl4tKYZW4u/view?usp=sharing" rel="noopener noreferrer">PDF</a>)
    </p>

    <p>
        In the public imagination, the world of scientists is often seen as a place of stimulating ideas, rich intellectual exchange, and exciting discoveries. While this image holds some truth, it overlooks a less visible reality. Behind the scenes, scientists must navigate rigid institutional hierarchies and administrative decisions that can be abrupt and deeply disruptive. Careers built over decades can be suddenly unsettled, with researchers reassigned, suspended, or removed from their work environments—even after more than twenty-five years in the same laboratory.
    <p>
    <p>
        Scientific institutions carry a dual responsibility: advancing knowledge while upholding the principles of fairness and justice that sustain trust in the research enterprise. When governance fails to balance these missions, the consequences extend far beyond individual cases. In recent years, a growing number of scientists across France have reported disciplinary and administrative procedures that raise serious concerns about transparency, due process, and proportionality. A collective of researchers and staff members has begun documenting these issues through O₂ER (Observatory for Oversight of Disciplinary Practices in Higher Education and Research). The reports we have gathered span multiple institutions and, although each case differs, they reveal recurring vulnerabilities: investigations perceived as predominantly accusatory; limited ability to present evidence or defense within a meaningful timeframe, or to ensure a thorough and exhaustive examination of the facts; blurred boundaries between reporting, investigation, and decision-making; and a lack of structured mediation before escalation. These weaknesses can transform professional disagreement into institutional breakdown.
    <p>
    <p>
        When problems arise, scientists are prepared to face accusations and accept the consequences of their actions. That expectation, however, collapses when institutions build cases on unverified or distorted evidence and fail to ensure effective contradiction. In many instances, allegations circulate through successive administrative layers, gaining legitimacy not through verification but through repetition. Senior authorities may then validate earlier conclusions simply because they have already been endorsed at lower levels. This dynamic reflects a well-documented cognitive mechanism: confirmation bias embedded within institutional processes.
    <p>
    <p>
        This is not an abstract concern: it is a lived reality. In such contexts, even experienced scientists can lose the ability to defend themselves effectively. The emotional toll is severe: humiliation, distress, harassment, resignation, and social isolation are commonly reported. Many begin to question their competence, reconsider their commitment to science, or contemplate leaving research altogether. At the same time, colleagues often withdraw—not out of indifference, but out of fear: fear of being associated with a contested case, fear of retaliation, fear of becoming the next target.
    <p>
    <p>
        The consequences extend well beyond individuals. Research teams are directly destabilized. Doctoral candidates and postdoctoral researchers can suddenly lose supervision, access to data, and continuity in their projects. In some cases, they are forced to relocate or interrupt their work entirely. At the level of research programs, the damage is equally tangible. Collaborative projects—especially those involving clinical data, sensitive infrastructures, or international partnerships—can stall or collapse under administrative constraints. Funding timelines are disrupted, and grant commitments are compromised. In major European programs, institutions may fail to meet their core obligation: ensuring that researchers have the conditions necessary to carry out their work.
    <p>
    <p>
        Unjust disciplinary decisions can create a profound sense of unreality, leaving scientists unsure of the reasons behind them and unclear about the evidence supporting them. What makes this particularly troubling is that such decisions often come from individuals who manage science rather than actively practice it, and are frequently triggered by peer conflicts in which competition, resource allocation, or personal stakes can bias initial reporting. 
    <p>
    <p>
        A particularly concerning dimension is the gendered nature of these processes. Women appear disproportionately targeted or discredited, sometimes through explicitly biased characterizations. Reports of female scientists being described as “too authoritative” or “too masculine,” criticized for allegedly overshadowing others’ expertise, or having their own expertise questioned, reveal how quickly legitimate scientific authority can be reframed as personal fault. In such cases, institutional procedures do not merely fail to correct bias: they actively reproduce it.
    <p>
    <p>
        Ultimately, these dynamics point to a structural problem. Disciplinary systems that lack clear separation of roles, timely access to evidence, and safeguards against retaliation risk becoming tools of escalation rather than resolution. They generate instability, weaken teams, and erode trust—not only within institutions but across the broader scientific ecosystem. In a European context that depends on cross-border collaboration, reproducibility, and shared standards, such governance failures carry strategic consequences: reduced attractiveness for international talent, fragilization of partnerships, and declining confidence in institutional integrity.
    <p>
    <p>
        This is not an argument against accountability. On the contrary, robust mechanisms to address misconduct are essential. But accountability without procedural fairness is not justice—it is arbitrariness. Ensuring effective contradiction, proportionality, and transparency is not optional; it is a prerequisite for legitimate decision-making.
    <p>
    <p>
        We therefore call for international attention and support to ensure that French institutions consistently apply the fundamental guarantees of French and European law: effective contradiction, timely access to evidence, reasoned decisions, and respect for legal and ethical standards. We also urge transparency regarding the composition and training of disciplinary bodies, particularly when decisions hinge on complex legal and scientific assessments. Clearer distinctions must be maintained between legitimate scientific expectations—including rigorous supervision, peer review, and publication standards—and allegations of misconduct. Conflating demanding scientific practice with harassment not only harms individuals; it degrades scientific standards and fosters a climate of fear, discouraging rigor and critical engagement. French research is already operating under severe resource constraints and continues to lose talent; it cannot afford governance mechanisms that further destabilize teams, undermine leading scientists, or jeopardize clinical and translational programs.
    <p>
    <p>
        If scientific institutions are to fulfill their mission, they must confront this imbalance directly. Protecting scientific research requires protecting the conditions under which science is produced—including the people who produce it. Without that, the cost is not only human. It is epistemic, institutional, and ultimately collective. Only by creating an environment that respects both scientific rigor and human dignity can research communities thrive, retain talent, and maintain public trust in science.
    <p>
    <p>

    </p>

    <p>
      Signed: On behalf of O₂ER (Observatory for Oversight of Disciplinary Practices in Higher Education and Research)
    </p>
